In the valley of Liro, prosperity rested on a single thread: reciprocity. When a gift traveled from one household to another, the recipient owed a return, not a repayment of equal value, but a pledge to pass forward kindness in kind, weaving a circle that warmed every doorstep.

The elders spoke of the First Exchange, when a hunter shared scraps with a widow, and the widow, in turn, fed the hungry neighbors. From that moment, corn grew in abundance and rivers ran clear, for the land mirrored the harmony of its people. The townsfolk built a communal granary, and every harvest was a shared festival; no door stayed shut, no feast declined. The pact was simple: honor the return of gifts, and prosperity returned to the giver a hundredfold.

But misfortune shadowed the valley when a trader named Kestrel claimed to have given much yet took more in secrecy. He kept the story of his generosity to himself, and quietly fostered debts without intention of repayment. The granary grew hollow, the wells ran thin, and the people’s hands grew wary of one another.

In time, Kestrel was confronted by the circle of elders and asked to name a single gift he had returned. His silence trembled like a reed in wind, and so he faced the burning of the festival lamp—the symbol that without reciprocal obligation, light must fade. One by one, the village renewed its promises, and prosperity bloomed again as each gift found its return, and betrayal learned to bow to the strength of shared generosity.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

